		<title type="html"><![CDATA[My exotic approach to GPU collision detection]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=2016#p2016"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>It all depends on the setup.<br><br>Usually the broadphase takes out most of the pairs, so 1024 sphere will never be a n^2 problem.<br></div></blockquote>But broadphase is n^2 (exact (n^2 - n)/2). No?<br>And it is not faster then sphere-sphere. Right?<br>So, it is only a broadphase will eat 16 ms for 1024 objects....</div></blockquote>No,<br><br>The CPU sweep and prune broadphase that Bullet uses is much cheaper. With typical motion (framecoherence) the cost roughly depends on the number of moving objects. For n objects, the cost is around O(n), but with small amount of moving objects it can be cheaper like O(m).<br><br>So it makes sense to run the broadphase on the CPU, and then upload a CollisionMap texture to GPU.<br><br>Erwin<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Sun Oct 01, 2006 8:02 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
